## The Erdős–Hajnal Theorem (`category research solved`)

`erdos_918.variants.erdos_hajnal`: For each finite k > 0, there exists a graph with ℵ_k vertices, χ = ℵ₁, and every induced subgraph on < ℵ_k vertices has χ ≤ ℵ₀.

For k = 1, the complete graph K_{ℵ₁} suffices: every induced subgraph on < ℵ₁ vertices is countable, hence χ ≤ ℵ₀. For general finite k, the Erdős–Hajnal construction uses a coloring f: [ℵ_k]² → ℵ₁ such that for every S ⊆ ℵ_k with |S| < ℵ_k, the range f[[S]²] is countable. Defining the graph by {α,β} ∈ E ↔ f(α,β) = 0 yields χ = ℵ₁ (using the ℵ₁-coloring from f) while every small induced subgraph is ℵ₀-colorable. The existence of such f is the deep part, established by Erdős and Hajnal via transfinite induction. I will not reconstruct the full proof here.

