## Textbook Theorems — Complete Proofs

### Theorem 1: `erdos_918.variants.eq_aleph_0.parts.i`

**Statement:** There does not exist a graph G on a vertex set V with #V = ℵ₂, χ(G) = ℵ₂, such that every induced subgraph on ℵ₁ vertices has chromatic number exactly ℵ₀.

**Proof.** Suppose for contradiction that such (V, G) exists.

**Case 1: G has an independent set I with |I| ≥ ℵ₁.**

Choose W ⊆ I with |W| = ℵ₁. Since I is independent, the induced subgraph G[W] has no edges, so χ(G[W]) = 1. But 1 ≠ ℵ₀, contradicting the assumption that every ℵ₁-vertex induced subgraph has chromatic number exactly ℵ₀.

**Case 2: Every independent set in G has size < ℵ₁ (equivalently, ≤ ℵ₀).**

Choose any W ⊆ V with |W| = ℵ₁. Suppose for contradiction that χ(G[W]) ≤ ℵ₀. Then there exists a proper coloring c: W → ℵ₀. For each i < ℵ₀, the color class C_i = c⁻¹({i}) is an independent set in G[W], hence an independent set in G. By the Case 2 assumption, |C_i| ≤ ℵ₀ for each i. Therefore:

|W| = |⋃_{i < ℵ₀} C_i| ≤ ∑_{i < ℵ₀} |C_i| ≤ ℵ₀ · ℵ₀ = ℵ₀.

But |W| = ℵ₁ > ℵ₀. This is a contradiction. Therefore χ(G[W]) > ℵ₀, so χ(G[W]) ≠ ℵ₀, again contradicting our assumption.

Both cases yield contradictions, so no such graph exists. □

**Key idea:** If there is a large independent set, a subset of it gives chromatic number 1. If all independent sets are small (≤ ℵ₀), then ℵ₀ colors can cover at most ℵ₀ · ℵ₀ = ℵ₀ vertices, which is fewer than ℵ₁ — so any ℵ₁-vertex induced subgraph requires more than ℵ₀ colors.

